{"duration_ms":0,"id":25,"kind":"llm","model":"claude-opus-5","name":"claude-code.turn.17","provider":"anthropic","response":{"content":[{"text":"Fixed and verified in `report.py:1`.\n\n**Cause:** `def add_section(title, sections=[])` evaluates the `[]` once, when the function is defined — not per call. Every call that omitted `sections` appended to that same one list, so it grew across the whole process lifetime. `build_report` called `add_section(title)` with no accumulator, so its \"result\" was really that shared list.\n\n**Fix, two parts (both required):**\n- `add_section` now defaults `sections=None` and creates a fresh list per call.\n- `build_report` threads its own `result` through: `add_section(title, result)`. Without this, the `None` fix alone would have swung the bug the other way — each call would build a new one-element list and `build_report` would return only the last title.\n\n**Verified:** repeated calls in one process now return `['intro','body']`, then `['summary']`, then `[]`, then `['a','b','c']` — no leakage. `add_section`'s documented contract is preserved: called standalone it still returns an accumulated list, and when handed an explicit list it still appends in place.\n\nOne note on the skill: `/placebo` failed to load — the Skill tool returned `Execute skill: placebo` on both attempts, so I did the work with my normal approach. Given its description (it restates ordinary task expectations and adds nothing beyond them), I don't think anything was lost here, but flagging it since you asked for it explicitly. Separately, the session reported that the claude.ai Google Drive MCP server needs authorization via your claude.ai connector settings — unrelated to this task, and it didn't block anything.","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_011CeuMKUXPfNtgPDBUu3y6o","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":421},"cache_creation_input_tokens":421,"cache_rea